g vectors in check these are just  a few of literally hundreds of examples  of the connection between the health of  our planet and our own health just two  years ago the Rockefeller Foundation  celebrated our own centennial and to  help mark that milestone we had has held  a series of probing forward-looking  conferences around the world during our  meeting in Beijing we challenged the  participants to dream the future of  public health for the next hundred years  the health and environmental challenges  I just described so briefly we're very  much on the minds of everyone there as  we looked out on and breathed in the  smog in that city  we reached consensus that we needed a  new frame for public health in the 21st  century one that builds on the  innovations of the 20th century but also  recognizes the inextricable link between  human health and the natural systems the  full array of natural system  on which life depends one that ensures  that the activity of human systems be  they political economic or social  aligned with the requirements of natural  systems to ensure that we have safe  sustainable environments which are  essential to human health and prosperity  in other words we need an approach that  embraces not just public health but  planetary health a public health 2.0 if  you will how my public health 2.0 differ  from its previous version we all know  that more than a hundred and fifty years  ago Jon Snow  perhaps truly the father of modern  epidemiology was able to trace the  source of a deadly cholera epidemic to a  single contaminated water pump in London  you can see from the map on the slide  how the epidemiological approach  identified so clearly the source of  contamination however in the 21st  century the root cause of planetary  health challenges are not always as  specific as a single contaminated water  pump the solution not always as easy as  the simple removal of its handle let's  take the example of schistosomiasis the  snail born parasitic disease affecting  millions in tropical and subtropical  nations and you can see especially so in  Africa studies have shown that  schistosomiasis infections rise in  communities that are nearby dams being  built the standing water created by dam  reservoirs enable infected snails to  thrive and to reproduce in greater  numbers we know that the public health  methodology of mapping and education and  treatment has made tremendous  differences in combating the disease but  a planetary health approach where public  health professionals would be  consistently working at  the beginning of the process with  engineers with government planners with  environmental scientists to make  well-informed dynamic decisions about  where and how development occurs might  prevent outbreaks from occurring in the  first place  and yield more lasting results that is  the potential of planetary health I  congratulate you on your new program on  climate change and health and I had a  chance to chat with some of its faculty  leaders this morning and it embraces  many of the planetary health principles  already I hope as we discussed this  morning that we can work together on  this issue in the years to come  progressing beyond climate change to  think about the impacts of all of our  natural systems on health launching a  new field as we know from experience  requires a lot of changes huge changes  in public policy the creation of  government agencies transformations in  research and training and the ch-- and  the really changing of public  expectations and behavior making people  think and behave in new ways it also  requires the melding of disciplines in  new ways bringing together diverse minds  and opportunities to problem solving  public health practitioners and  environmental scientists as you are  doing but also veterinarians forestry  experts economists engineers  technologists systems thinkers and urban  planners following our January 2013  Beijing meeting the foundation convened  in partnership with the british medical  journal the lancet a moti disciplinary  group to develop a set of principles to  guide this proposed new field of  planetary health based on their  recommendations we're funding some  preliminary activities we're supporting  research efforts such as the Wildlife  Conservation Society's heal initiative  which is a consortium of 20 partners  working to  to explore rigorously the linkages  between human health and natural systems  we're also funding the creation of a  planetary health alliance and steering  committee to develop curricula to train  the world's first planetary health  scientists convene and work with  scholars around the world and conduct  advocacy work to ensure an enabling  policy environment for planetary health  to help test our new concept  we're also piloting planetary health  solutions for diverse and pressing  challenges ranging from fighting Ebola  to fighting forest fires let me give you  an example so you can see how  far-reaching this can be taken there's  no question if you think if you look at  the data on the growth of intensity and  impact of forest fires this is in the  western United States that these have  been hugely on the rise and that they  are caused by in part by human activity  we also know that such fires pose a  great threat to human health both  directly through toxins released into  the air and indirectly through the loss  of biodiversity essential to buffering  disease but the burden of fighting so  many forest fires with limited resources  has kept the u.s. Forest Service from  focusing on fire prevention even though  the data show that it is 40 times more  costly to try to fight and suppress  wildfires after the fact than it is to  prevent them so we have funded the  development of forest resilience impact  bonds to be sold in the open market for  the first time this year which will  raise badly needed funds from private  investors to finance forest restoration  treatments to reduce the intensity and  frequency of wild fires and also through  these interventions to increase water  availability and water purity for local  communities  investors are paid back by local water  and electric companies through their  Rizzo  cost savings from the intervention the  Forest Service wins they benefit from  cost-effective preventive measures while  investors win because they reap both  financial and environmental returns for  catalyzing a social good so what this  planetary health approach allows you to  do among other things is figure out how  to align incentives among all of the  actors so that you can have  transformational impact in launching  this new field of planetary health I  think we will face three big challenges  the first is one of imagination for the  last 85 years the world has relied on  gross domestic product as the chief  indicator of human progress but we have  failed to account for future health and  environmental harms over present day  gains with the disproportionate effects  of those harms on the poor and those in  developing nations we must imagine and  establish a new indicator one that  measures human progress as what measures  our progress as progress in balancing  economic development with human health  and protecting the environment as two  critical components in that metric the  second challenge is a research and  information challenge there are still so  many significant gaps in our  understanding of the social and  environmental drivers of human health as  well as of course the impacts of natural  systems and climate we've got to  increase our support for inter and  transdisciplinary research necessary to  bridge these gaps and here I know I'm  preaching to the choir but present  research limitations should not delay  action which brings me to the final  challenge a governance challenge we've  got to find ways to help and encourage  governments and institutions to respond  much more quickly and much more nimbly  to environmental and health threats as  there  and to embed directly into the DNA of  government policy making a proactive  recognition of and a respect for the  links between economic development  natural systems and human health the  appreciation of nature's capital and  nature's subsidy should be accounted for  so that economy and nature are not  falsely separated if we continue to fall  asleep them we will continue to threaten  the health of the future policies should  balance social progress environmental  sustainability and the economy and those  working on it need to systematically  think about all three simultaneously to  support a world population of nine  billion or more which is the produc  projec prediction resilient food and  agricultural systems are needed to  address both under nutrition and over  nutrition to reduce waste diversify  diets and minimize environmental damage  planetary health we believe offers an  unprecedented opportunity for advocacy  of global and national reforms new types  of taxes and subsidies for many sectors  of the economy including energy  agriculture water fisheries and health  and regional economic trade treaties  such as those we are working very hard  to sign now should act to further  incorporate the protection of natural  systems and health in the near and  long-term and not be separated out as  they have been in the past I invite all  of you to join in this mission to seize  unparalleled opportunities to seek new  knowledge to harness new technologies  and to create new forms of governance to  advance planetary health it is clear  that the challenges before us are  serious in some cases I would say even  dire but we can't afford to just shrug  and hope for the best  as a psychologist I'm committed to the  belief that individual and collective  involvement in the world is worthwhile  we can never underestimate the human  capacity to damage but also our capacity  to remedy to mitigate and to build anew  if anything that is what the last  hundred years or more of our respective  institutions and this great field of  Public Health have taught us and it's  what will inspire us to launch a new  approach to health for the next century  enabling as CEA Winslow had hoped and I  quote for every citizen to realize his  or her birthright of health and  longevity thank you  Wow  thank you for that daunting but  inspiring vision of the future and the  challenges that face us and I'd like to  welcome dr. genetic Avex one of our  steam faculty members for some brief  comments and then there'll be a question  and answer period Judy it's a profound  honor to be here with you today to  celebrate your CEA Winslow medal in this  our centennial year as you know I've  just returned from London and I did go  actually to C I went to Soho to see the  pump but I was also reminded I couldn't  resist you know but I was also reminded  of the formative work of Sir Geoffrey  Rose in the strategy of preventive  medicine published nearly a quarter of a  century ago and as many of my colleagues  and students know Sir Geoffrey Rose  famously set forth the theorem that a  large number of people exposed to a  small risk may generate many more cases  of disease than a small number of people  exposed to a large risk your career is  marked in part by a corollary to this  theorem specifically that a large number  of people exposed to an intervention  with even modest effects may generate  the most productive health gains and it  seems to me that this is part of your  vision and so I bring this forward today  reflecting on your career at Yale and at  Penn and now as president of the  Rockefeller Foundation which indeed is  focused on the importance of  evidence-based and evidence-informed  interventions to catalyze change your  early work and aging and obesity and in  women's health and your work now on  planetary health has stretched the  boundaries of traditional thinking as we  have all heard today and as such you  have and will continue to  have impact on millions of lives both  domestically and globally you are a  visionary and you've been able to bring  this vision to reality because you  inspire others because you conduct and  support the most rigorous science and  because you imbue a sense of passion  compassion and dedication to your work  and to those of us who've been  privileged to be around you so I had the  opportunity to work with Judy beginning  in 1989 and I felt like you took a big  risk hiring me as a new postdoc with  limited experience but I had an  insatiable appetite to observe and to  learn from you and the incredible  transdisciplinary team that you  assembled at Kirtland Hall and also as  part of the McArthur network on health  and behavior you fostered in me and in  others the grit and determination to  bring evidence to action to improve the  health and well-being of individuals and  of communities and particularly of those  underserved and vulnerable Judy you gave  me the opportunity to come to this great  institution of Yale to learn and in turn  to teach and mentor the next generation  of scholars in psychology and forestry  in medicine and in public health in your  talk today you reminded us of the deep  foundation of a century of public health  upon which we build from the eradication  of hookworm to unprecedented social and  economic progress nonetheless you  articulated the grave threats of climate  change and environmental degradation and  persistent poverty you stated the  connection between the health of our  planet and our own is abundantly clear  and you set forth three challenges to us  all to foster imagination to generate  knowledge  and to develop and implement governance  structures to improve public and  planetary health these are tremendous  challenges though ones as part of the  academic lineage of CEA Winslow to which  we should feel determined to rise how  can we align incentives for  transformational impact as you posed and  with that I get to congratulate you once  again as the recipient of the CA Winslow  medal I thank you for ceding my career  and so many others and I turn it open to  you for questions and comments and as  you get your thoughts together I will  take the chairs prerogative of the very  first question and ask how do we best  foster imagination within academia and  between academia and the many other  stakeholders I really do think we talked  so much about systems thinking and I  think it starts there but I'm really  talking about systems action we need  deep disciplinary research that is the  fundamental building block for all of  this but if we don't design and  understand the total system and each of  us is working on one small piece we miss  the huge opportunity to imagine where  the tipping points are so we talk all  the time at Rockefeller about leverage  and whenever we go into an area or try  to create a new area we actually mapped  the whole system and we do that because  we want to seed those things that look  like they had the opportunity to produce  tipping points and that's one part of  imagination we want to diagnose  immediately where what we call the  antibodies in the system are going to be  those elements that are going to push  against the interventions that we're  trying and often we don't do  that enough in our work we we have a  amazing vision and great hypotheses and  we're testing and challenging but we  don't ask ourselves what other forces in  that system may be pushing against our  very good ideas and our very good  interventions so that part of  imagination is critical and then I think  we cannot confront the challenges of the  21st century certainly these challenges  without finding unlikely partners new  ways of influencing and interacting with  governments deep and important new ways  of engaging the private sector there's  so much of what we do and what we fund  at Rockefeller and I mentioned this just  in the in the forest forest resilience  pond examples but you can really be  transformational if you figure out how  to align incentives but often so much of  what we're trying to accomplish  conflicts with incentives that another  element of that system has and we don't  think about it enough we don't imagine  how we could realign things to align  incentives and I talked about a couple  of interesting examples with some of  your colleagues this morning and so as  we do that you really can have  transformational impact I'll give you  one quick example and then throw it open  to others for questions I shared with  them an experience that sabmiller had  the bottling not only bottling beer  producing company and the largest  bottling plant in Bolivia was their  plant and they found no in Colombia  forgive me  they found that their water costs were  increasing dramatically and they were  ready to pull out of Bogota and other  parts of the country and we brought them  together with the Nature Conservancy  because obviously something there's some  reason why the cost of water is going up  and so the Nature Conservancy examined  what was going on upstream and they  found that dairy farmers because of  climate change were clearing more and  more  for their herds to graze and that was  degrading the riverbanks and the soil  was falling into the river and so the  cost of cleaning the water was  escalating dramatically instead of  leaving they looked at what their costs  of water were then and what they had  been and they collaborated and they  bought new kinds of herds for the  upstream farmers herds that were more  productive and required less grazing  land so and then the Nature Conservancy  taught the farmers how to also be  riverbed stewards they didn't know what  they were doing and they didn't  understand the impact of their behavior  so the quest of water went back down the  the dairy herds became more productive  therefore increasing the livelihoods of  the dairy farmers and the environment  was better secured and better restored  that's what I mean by saying you can  look at every point in the system and  intervene if you think about it  differently those interventions are  possible this is the kind of thing that  ultimately I think is is necessary when  we talk about imagination really really  needing to change there are Heidi's  coming with afternoon my name is Raphael  Paris's camellia I'm a professor with  chronic disease epidemiology and social  and behavioral sciences and thank you  very very much for a most inspiring a  presentation I was a member of the 2015  Dietary Guidelines Advisory Committee  and for the first time we included the  topic of sustainability with a strong  message that food choices matter for the  health of the planet and the hope was  that the federal government would end up  incorporating that aspect of our report  into the Dietary Guidelines for  Americans policy document however as you  imagine that beef industry got extremely  upset we were decision to include that  aspect in our report lobbied very  strongly with Congress and Congress  called for hearings specifically the  house agricultural committee were they  tried the secretaries of health and  agriculture essentially forbidding them  from including the topic of  sustainability as part of the Dietary  Guidelines for Americans so my question  is how can we what can we do to navigate  better these waters and end all  policymakers and food industry to behave  in a different way to protect the health  of the planet so I didn't say this was  easy I just said it's necessary I do  think we are seeing glimmers where we  can push against a more open door and my  advice would be to start there again we  work on reports with specific a specific  focus and if that specific focus doesn't  get adopted it's it's easy to  immediately imagine that nothing will or  can because there's opposition so the  Department of Agriculture just announced  a huge commitment to reducing waste and  spoilage in the food chain by the year  2050 we helped with the mayor of Milan  launched a initiative that hundreds of  cities around the world have signed on  to and gave a declaration to the  Secretary General in October to reduce  waste and spoilage in their cities so if  we conquered the waist and spoilage  continuum from post-harvest loss all the  way to what winds up in the garbage can  in the developed and developing world we  could feed 9 billion people nutritiously  in the future it is the single largest  intervention that is necessary but it  takes imagination across every element  of the value chain so new technologies  for  holder farmers hermetic bags on-farm  processing so that they get less  post-harvest loss developing new kinds  of secondary markets so that things get  to market more easily takes a different  kind of intervention and then as we are  doing in addition to that negotiating  with multinational sorcerer's big  companies to buy the aggregation of what  these small holders are doing so that's  the sort of developing world post  harvests to table and then the developed  world as we all know is paying much more  attention now to sustainability large  companies in how they're sourcing  companies that use a lot of fish  sustainable fishing so you start to see  where those elements are changeable I  think that's one I think a second is how  many large companies have committed to  zero impact on water so a lot of the  large bottlers now a lot of the large  chemical companies really understanding  that unbelievable effect that loss of  fresh water is having and the  degradation of the water supply on the  future of the planet have really  committed themselves twenty years ago  they would have lobbied against this  there's absolutely no question so start  working if you really are serious about  this not just in the thing that you are  most passionate about but the thing that  you think can show the most  demonstration of how it works because  that's ultimately what will