eally helped to produce this Venn  decline and this rapid decline that  we're seeing and again I want to  re-emphasize so much that this is way  more than climate change this is a total  loss of biodiversity on which everything  in the environment and then everything  in human health depend and so we've got  to get back down to the root causes and  many of these variables as we start to  approach this problem we break for lunch  we'll take one more go across the street  for lunch and the economist dr. Jody  Sindelar had her hand up earlier he  seemed to queue her up great professor  here in the School of Public Health and  health policy and management and I  someone answered my question but I  appreciate that you expanded the view of  what is public health to planetary  health but I was wondering if one of the  key segments or points of your analysis  should be emphasizing social issues more  not just social as we all know and I  know you know well social determinants  of health but also social outcomes and  maybe you answered that a little bit  when you talked about different ways of  measuring well-being so just that's my  basically right so absolutely the way  this is being formulated social  variables are both independent variables  and dependent variables in this process  so we know and of course Michael Marmot  spoke to this group about the social  determinants of health but social  outcomes are determined by these natural  systems and by what we are doing in  terms of natural systems so who is hurt  more for example we we at Rockefeller do  an enormous amount of work on resilience  as I think you know and if you look at  who's most affected by the shocks and  stresses that are coming with increasing  frequency whether they're tsunamis and  typhoons or hurricanes or it's always  the poor and most vulnerable both  because of where they live  and because of their capacity to their  limit more limited capacity to rebound  more effectively so what's great about  t